# Sessions hold run state, blobs and whatever the agent wrote.
# None of it is source.
sessions/

# The same, one level up: a batch's workspaces, memory copies and answers.
batches/

# Memory is worth keeping: `memory.db` and `files/` are what the agents
# learned, vectors included, and nothing rebuilds them. The database is folded
# into that one file whenever the last run using it closes; the write-ahead
# log beside it is runtime state, as is a version 1 memory's lock.
memory/memory.db-wal
memory/memory.db-shm
memory/.lock

# Scratch, inside the workspace so the sandbox can reach it.
.tmp/

# Credentials, and the ones test chats use instead.
.env
.env.test

# A rag index commits everything except its vectors: they are binary, they are
# large, and `zen rag <subject> restore` rebuilds them from the copies of the
# documents the index already carries — which is why this is safe to ignore and
# `memory/` above is not. A clone therefore holds an index that looks built and
# cannot search until a setup step restores it. `zen rag <subject> ready` is the
# test for that; `[ -f manifest.json ]` is not, because the manifest is here.
assets/**/lance/

.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc